      "c-98142b931f680d46": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-634",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "See also Luke 52:17. The grain in the East is threshed in the open air (see note on Gen. 50:10 #90) by being trampled under the feet of oxen or horses (see note on Deut. 25:4) or by means of instruments, as described in the note on Isaiah 28:27-28 (#508). By these processes the s…",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-634",
          "sourceHash": "b9f39da0d2cdbe60a4be6bac486f62013a0a7032580b2786b4186f799c5b1ad3",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      },
      "c-caca67671f60d9fc": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-633",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "To carry the master’s sandals was considered the most menial duty that could be performed. On entering a house the sandals are taken off by a servant, who takes care of them, and brings them again when needed. In India it is customary for a servant to accompany his master when he…",
        "traduccion": {
